data: {"id":"chatcmpl-6fec93f7-5d56-4aa9-9e12-a57e387e1811","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795544431,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"Initial checksum validation uses Fletcher-16 for data integrity checks within log entries, comparing calculated checksums against stored values to detect and handle data corruption. If checksums mismatch, either a `WalException` is thrown, or, if the end of the log is reached, a potentially incomplete record is identified.\n\nReplay phase uses a deterministic LinkedHashMap for state, ordered TreeMap for open transactions (to facilitate replay order), and a HashSet to track seen transaction IDs. This structure sets the stage for implementing transaction replay logic, including conflict resolution based on transaction history. R22's requirements are under consideration to avoid errors.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

data: {"id":"chatcmpl-6fec93f7-5d56-4aa9-9e12-a57e387e1811","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795544431,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"U committed, then did `ROLLBACK`, the write to key `k` would *not* count when comparing T's read set to U's writes at commit. The rule specifies the \"at the time it was committed\" to exclude keys subsequently rolled back. A conflict occurs if there's an overlap between T's read set and U's committed write set.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

data: {"id":"chatcmpl-6fec93f7-5d56-4aa9-9e12-a57e387e1811","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795544431,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"The analysis confirms the key `\"k\"` is indeed part of transaction U's committed write set, aligning precisely with the specification's definition. Section 4 explicitly includes both PUT and DELETE operations, and R22's conflict condition hinges on overlap with T's read set.\n\nPhase 2 will be traced, checking R13 through R19d in order.  Calculations for the payload layout will use `pay = p + 3`. R13's checks will depend on the record type and involve multiple length checks to validate record structure, ensuring data integrity across various transaction types (BEGIN, COMMIT, ABORT, PUT, DELETE). Malformed payloads throw exceptions.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

data: {"id":"chatcmpl-6fec93f7-5d56-4aa9-9e12-a57e387e1811","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795544431,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"Detailed length checks are crucial, accounting for transaction type-specific data layouts. Each record type necessitates distinct validation: certain types require specific field counts while others enforce minimum lengths and conditional field validations, throwing a specific exception for malformed payloads. Transaction ID validation, particularly for records other than CHECKPOINT, requires checks for a non-zero transaction ID.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

data: {"id":"chatcmpl-6fec93f7-5d56-4aa9-9e12-a57e387e1811","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795544431,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"For transaction IDs, a zero ID is invalid and throws an exception. Rules R15, R16, and R16a specify data validation for keys, values, and names, ensuring they fall within defined ranges and throwing exceptions for malformed data. Rule R17 checks for duplicate transaction IDs during BEGIN, and R18 addresses an open transaction limit.\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

